  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened Humanitarian Response Adapted to the Needs of People Affected by the Drought in the South of Madagascar</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Population movements in the southern region of Madagascar are recurrent cyclical phenomena, as an adaptation strategy applied by households to escape the hazards of agricultural life, which is strongly impacted by the progressive deterioration of environmental conditions. It is also a known phenomenon that departures are accentuated during difficult periods of drought. Now, the period of drought that has affected the Deep South since 2019 has taken on exceptional proportions, with 36 per cent of the region classified as severe drought (alarm) and 1 per cent as extreme drought (emergency). This situation requires an immediate humanitarian response, to address, among other things, difficulties in agricultural production, malnutrition, the intensification of gender-based violence, but also the sudden displacement of the population.Through this project, IOM is implementing its Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) in order to strengthen an adapted response to protection, nutrition, food security, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), emergency shelter, etc. needs through regular collection, analysis and sharing of information on the number of displaced persons, their profiles, vulnerabilities and needs, in four targeted districts (Amboasary, Ambovombe, Tsihombe, Ampanihy).</narrative>
  </description>
